@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@cindex commands
Commands group into configuration/inspection, device, compute and development.
Vector @var{SRC} arguments and output options are described under
@ref{Invocation}.

@section Configuration and inspection

@deffn Command modes
@deffnx Command devices
@deffnx Command pipelines
@deffnx Command io
@deffnx Command config
List the loaded configuration: the modes (name, pattern, doc), device profiles
(name, port), pipelines (with their stages), I/O profiles, or---for
@command{config}---the resolved user directory, the files that were actually
loaded, and the entity counts.
@end deffn

@deffn Command check
Validate the loaded configuration: compile every mode and resolve every
pipeline stage to a defined mode, reporting any errors.  Exit status is
non-zero if anything fails.  Run it after editing a @file{.cgra} file.
@end deffn

@deffn Command init [--force]
Install the built-in defaults to @file{~/.config/cgra/config.cgra} and copy the
bundled standard library into @file{~/.config/cgra/stdlib/}.  Refuses to
overwrite an existing config unless @option{--force} is given.
The source is @env{CGRA_STDLIB} when set, otherwise the configured installed
directory or a source-tree fallback (@pxref{Environment}). Missing library
files or copy/write failures return nonzero. Initialization is not
transactional: a failure can leave defaults or some library files written.
@end deffn

@deffn Command show mode [--imm @var{N}] [-v]
Print the compiled 32-bit configuration words for @var{mode}.  With
@option{-v}, each word is decoded into a readable per-PE line
(@code{op a=@dots{} b=@dots{} imm=@dots{}}).  @option{--imm} overrides the
immediate of any @code{const} operand before compiling.
@end deffn

@section Device

@deffn Command ping
Identify the device and print its protocol version and geometry; warns if the
reported geometry or protocol is unsupported.
@end deffn

@deffn Command probe
With no device selected, scan @file{/dev/ttyUSB*} and @file{/dev/ttyACM*}, ping
each, report any CGRA found, and record the first (usable later as
@samp{-d auto}).  With a device selected, identify it and run the known-answer
bring-up tests---the FPGA acceptance check.
@end deffn

@deffn Command reset
Clear the PE registers; the loaded configuration is preserved.
@end deffn

@deffn Command dump
Read back all R x C PE registers and print them, one array row per line.
@end deffn

@section Compute

@deffn Command run mode [@var{nums}@dots{}] [--a @var{SRC}] [--b @var{SRC}] [--imm @var{N}] [--steps @var{N}]
Run an element-wise or reduction @var{mode} over the input vector(s).
Element-wise modes read @var{a} from the north edge and (for binary ops)
@var{b} from the west, producing one output per input.  Reduction modes
(@code{pattern = reduce}) fold the stream into a single scalar.  @option{--imm}
sets a @code{const} operand; @option{--steps} overrides the mode's step count.
Step counts must be complete integer literals in 0..255 and immediates in
-32768..65535. Invalid values are rejected rather than truncated.
@end deffn

@deffn Command pipe name [@var{nums}@dots{}] [--a @var{SRC}] [--b @var{SRC}]
Run a pipeline: the first stage consumes @var{a} (and @var{b} if it is binary),
and each stage's output becomes the next stage's input.  Per-stage immediates
come from the pipeline definition (@code{stage = muli imm=3}).
@end deffn

@deffn Command matvec -m @var{SRC} --x @var{SRC} [--cols @var{N}]
Systolic matrix--vector product @math{y = A x}.  @var{A} is row-major; its width
defaults to the length of @var{x} (or @option{--cols}).  Uses the
weight-stationary dataflow, tiled over rows x cols blocks for any @code{M x N}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command conv -m @var{KERNEL} --x @var{SIGNAL}
Full 1-D convolution @code{out[i] = sum_k h[k]*x[i-k]} of length
@code{n+k-1}, realised as a matrix--vector with a Toeplitz matrix.
@end deffn

@deffn Command scan [@var{nums}@dots{}] [--a @var{SRC}] [--op sum|max|min|prod]
Inclusive prefix scan on row 0: @code{out[i] = a[0] op @dots{} op a[i]}, tiled
over columns with the running fold carried between blocks.  Default op is
@code{sum}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command bench [@var{mode}] [--size @var{N}] [--repeat @var{R}] [--json]
Measure one mode (default @code{add}) with one checked warm-up and @var{R}
measured calls. Every call is checked outside API timing. Independent scalar
references cover stateless diagonals, reductions, the structurally recognized
custom ADD example, matvec, scan and convolution. Matvec uses @var{N} rows and
16 columns; scan uses @var{N} inputs; convolution uses @var{N} inputs and five
coefficients, producing @var{N}+4 outputs. This benchmark output capacity is
separate from the regular vector CLI limit. @var{N} is 1..4096 and @var{R}
1..INT_MAX, subject to timing-sample allocation. Unsupported references fail
an individual @command{bench} invocation with an explicit skip.
@end deffn

@deffn Command benchall [--size @var{N}] [--repeat @var{R}] [--json]
Report every loaded mode as @code{passed}, @code{failed} or @code{skipped}.
Malformed mappings, transport errors and numerical mismatches fail. Arbitrary
custom and stateful-diagonal modes without an independent reference are
skipped; being compilable is not verification. The known 4x4 ADD template is
recognized by all PE operations/routes, output selection and schedule, never
by its name. Its fixed coordinates are invalid on incompatible geometries.

JSON schema version 2 records backend, geometry, protocol, baud/timeout,
compiler, actual build signatures, source hashes, commit/dirty state when
available, platform and deterministic workload ID. @env{CGRA_BITSTREAM_ID}
is an optional operator-supplied identifier, not device attestation; absence
is JSON null. @code{compiled_template_words} records compilation output;
matvec/conv fill weights per tile rather than using those zero placeholders.

Each successful entry contains API/CPU samples in measurement order, mean,
median and nearest-rank p95. The CPU baseline executes the scalar reference
with identical modular semantics, consumes its outputs and includes reference
dispatch overhead; it is not a claim of optimal native CPU performance.
@code{prepare_ms} covers compile/reference/sample allocation once per mode.
@code{configuration_io_ms} and @code{execution_io_ms} sum instrumented transport
calls over measured iterations, including retries and emulator work or serial
waits. @code{host_api_other_ms} is remaining API wall time (host computation,
serialization, checking and instrumentation overhead). @code{verify_ms}
covers API/CPU comparisons outside both timed computations. Workload filling,
ID and warm-up are excluded from measured link counters. Profiling adds clock
calls; these measurements cannot be converted into FPGA speedup.

Byte/transaction counters, configuration transactions and requested RUN/EXEC
steps are separate from useful arithmetic counts. The latter count scalar
adds/subtracts/multiplies, exclude routing/padding and initial zero additions,
and are null when no count is specified. Requested steps do not attest physical
captures after a failed transfer. Failed entries retain completed samples and
a reason. Totals include successful measurements; any failure or zero successes
returns nonzero. Skips are explicit in @code{results} and the summary.

Completed reports are published with @option{-o} even on failure; failures
before reporting preserve the destination. @command{make bench} executes each
requested size once, stores its complete JSON in an isolated run directory,
and publishes a schema-2 sweep index at @env{OUT} (default
@file{sw/build/benchmark.json}). The index lists point size, command, exit
status, path and hash. It preserves failed reports and incomplete diagnostics;
if no new report is produced, the previous public index remains untouched.
The run directory and index must be distributed together. @env{DEV=auto}
first probes the device. Source hashes and samples make evidence traceable,
not a proof of all possible workload values.
@end deffn

@section Development

@deffn Command emulate
Serve the in-process emulator on a freshly allocated pseudo-terminal and print
its path; point any serial tool---or another @command{cgra} process via
@code{-d /dev/pts/N}---at it.  Runs until interrupted.
@end deffn

@deffn Command selftest
Run every kernel and pattern against the @samp{sim:} emulator, checking each
against an independent host computation, and exercise the retry path with
@samp{sim:flaky}.  Exit status reflects the failures.
@end deffn
